1079 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (The robot should maintain two internal power lines: a high-current motor line and a clean logic line. Keep these electrically isolated except at the battery source. Use common grounds, but route heavy motor grounds separately to prevent noise feedback into the microcontroller. For SAR image transmission, the ESP32 or Raspberry Pi should form an HTTP/MQTT client sending bundled JSON + image payloads to the local server. The onboard architecture must handle task scheduling: motor control loop, encoder counting, and camera capture must not block each other. On ESP32, dual-core allocation or timed tasks \(e.g., FreeRTOS\) are ideal. On Arduino, offload camera to a separate MCU.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1080 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 278 0 R
/T (Wiring, Safety & Reliability)
/E (Wiring, Safety & Reliability)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1081 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (All wires on a crawler bot must withstand movement, shaking, and repeated impacts from obstacles. Use stranded silicone wires, not rigid ones. Keep wiring harnesses short and grouped using heat-shrink or cable sleeves. Any connection prone to disconnection \(Dupont jumper wires\) should be avoided or glued in place. Solder joints must be reinforced, and connectors should have mechanical strain relief. Place the main switch in an accessible location and include a simple fuse or polyfuse if running higher current systems. Before competing, every team should perform a vibration test: hang the robot on a rope, manually shake the rope, and confirm that no electronics reset or power drops occur.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1082 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 278 0 R
/T (Recommended Control Architectures)
/E (Recommended Control Architectures)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1083 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 278 0 R
/E (A dual-processor setup is ideal: an ESP32 or Arduino handling motion + encoders, and an ESP32-CAM handling imaging and network uploads. For advanced teams, a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W can unify everything but must be thermally managed and given a stable 5V supply. The final architecture must ensure deterministic timing for motor control and data reporting.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
